# Line endings are pinned here, and for this repository that is a correctness
# requirement rather than a formatting preference.
#
# The fixtures under `fixtures/` are compared byte for byte against documents the engine
# rebuilds from the same model - `integration-tests/src/corpus.rs::assert_committed` reads
# the committed file and requires it to equal the generated string exactly. The
# specification states its determinism requirement over bytes, so a working tree that
# rewrites line endings is not the same tree.
#
# Nothing pinned the working-tree form, and Git's default on Windows - Git for Windows
# sets `core.autocrlf=true` - rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. So every committed fixture
# arrived at the Windows runner one byte longer per line than the document the engine
# produces, and two suites in `integration-tests/contracts` failed with "the files differ
# only in length or trailing newline". It reported the fixtures as stale, which is the
# wrong diagnosis, and it did so only on the one platform whose default disagreed.
#
# The defect was unreachable until `.github/workflows/rust.yml` was corrected to run the
# workspace suite: its test step named the root package, which has no tests, so the
# three-platform matrix had never run these suites anywhere.
#
# `text=auto` keeps Git's own binary detection, so anything it classifies as binary - a
# committed WebAssembly module, a captured response with a non-text byte - is still stored
# and checked out verbatim. Every file currently committed is text, so this changes no
# blob; it changes what Git writes into a working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The PowerShell installer is read by Windows PowerShell, which is the one place a CRLF
# would be conventional. It is deliberately left to the rule above: the script is
# ASCII text with LF endings today, PowerShell 5.1 and later read it correctly, and a
# per-file exception would make this file's promise ("one form everywhere") false.
